-- title:
--   Lemma 5.5 — monotonicity of the Bellman operator and lower/upper certificates
-- statement:
--   For $p\in\{1,2\}$, let $P$ be a player-$p$ MDP with a reward function (values in $[0,1]$) and a discount factor $\lambda\in[0,1)$, and let $\rho,\rho':S\to\mathbb R$. Then
--   1. (Monotonicity) if $\rho\preceq\rho'$ then $\Psi_P(\rho)\preceq\Psi_P(\rho')$;
--   2. (Lower bound) if $\rho\preceq\Psi_P(\rho)$ then $\rho\preceq\mathrm{val}_\lambda$;
--   3. (Upper bound) if $\Psi_P(\rho)\preceq\rho$ then $\mathrm{val}_\lambda\preceq\rho$.
--
--   Here $\mathrm{val}_\lambda$ is the value of the discounted-sum objective in $P$, defined from the expectation over all strategies, and $\preceq$ is the pointwise order. Items 2–3 are what make shrinking interval bounds certificates for MDP values.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a finite game G = (S, A, B, δ) with |S| = n and at most m actions per player → states Fin n, actions Fin mA and Fin mB with n, mA, mB ≥ 1, m = max(mA, mB), distributions as nonnegative real vectors summing to one; reason: relabelling of finite sets; nonempty state and action sets are the source game convention
--   - encoding: the probability measure on valid plays Ω_s generated by finite histories, strategies on valid histories → the Ionescu-Tulcea trajectory measure on all sequences in (S × A × B)^ℕ; strategies are defined on all finite stage lists; reason: invalid plays have probability zero and values of strategies on invalid histories never matter, so every expectation and value is unchanged
--   - encoding: a player-p MDP P = (S, A, δ) for p ∈ {1, 2} → a game with the other player's action set Fin 1 (CSG n m 1 for p = 1, CSG n 1 m for p = 2); the theorem is the conjunction of the two cases; reason: the source defines a player-p MDP as the special CSG where the other player has one action
-- source:
--   Ali Asadi, Krishnendu Chatterjee, The Computational Complexity of Concurrent Stochastic Games: Exact Value and Approximation, STOC 2027 submission #285 (October 2026), SHA-256 5cff187cf6b230b3bdfe540f2832f824d1ddb6acb43239fbf469d37c43b0d461, https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/285.pdf, Lemma 5.5, printed p. 14 (PDF p. 16)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Game
open Finset

namespace ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx

/-- **Lemma 5.5 (Properties of the Bellman operator).** For a player-`p` MDP `P` (`p ∈ {1,2}`) with
a reward function and a discount factor `λ ∈ [0,1)`, and `ρ, ρ' : S → ℝ`:
1. if `ρ ⪯ ρ'` then `Ψ_P(ρ) ⪯ Ψ_P(ρ')`;
2. if `ρ ⪯ Ψ_P(ρ)` then `ρ ⪯ val_λ`;
3. if `Ψ_P(ρ) ⪯ ρ` then `val_λ ⪯ ρ`. -/
theorem lemma_5_5 :
    (∀ {n m : ℕ} (P : CSG n m 1) (r : Reward n m 1), IsReward r → ∀ lam : ℝ, 0 ≤ lam → lam < 1 →
      ∀ ρ ρ' : Fin n → ℝ,
        (ρ ≤ ρ' → bellman₁ P r lam ρ ≤ bellman₁ P r lam ρ') ∧
        (ρ ≤ bellman₁ P r lam ρ → ρ ≤ discValue P r lam) ∧
        (bellman₁ P r lam ρ ≤ ρ → discValue P r lam ≤ ρ)) ∧
    (∀ {n m : ℕ} (P : CSG n 1 m) (r : Reward n 1 m), IsReward r → ∀ lam : ℝ, 0 ≤ lam → lam < 1 →
      ∀ ρ ρ' : Fin n → ℝ,
        (ρ ≤ ρ' → bellman₂ P r lam ρ ≤ bellman₂ P r lam ρ') ∧
        (ρ ≤ bellman₂ P r lam ρ → ρ ≤ discValue P r lam) ∧
        (bellman₂ P r lam ρ ≤ ρ → discValue P r lam ≤ ρ)) := by sorry

end ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx
